Make temploadfem.f's call to cload match cload's actual signature.

CalculiX 2.22 declares the user subroutine with 13 arguments:

    subroutine cload(xload,kstep,kinc,time,node,idof,coords,vold,
   &  mi,ntrans,trab,inotr,veold)

Three of its four callers -- tempload.f, tempload_em.f, temploaddiff.f -- pass exactly those
13. temploadfem.f passes 17, adding nmethod,idummy,ddummy,ddummy. On a native Fortran build
that is harmless: there are no cross-file prototypes and the callee simply ignores the extra
arguments.

wasm is strictly typed, so it is not harmless here. wasm-ld cannot reconcile the two
signatures and routes the mismatching call through a TRAPPING stub:

    wasm-ld: warning: function signature mismatch: cload_
    >>> defined as (i32 x 17) -> void in libccx.a(f_temploadfem.o)
    >>> defined as (i32 x 13) -> void in libccx.a(f_cload.o)

So reaching that line -- the *CLOAD user-subroutine path -- aborts the solver with
"unreachable" rather than calling anything. FreeCAD writes ordinary *CLOAD cards and does not
use the user-subroutine form, so this is latent rather than live, but it is a real trap sitting
in a linked module and the fix costs one line.

Truncating to 13 is semantically identical on every platform: the callee never had access to
arguments 14-17, because its dummy argument list stops at veold.

Found in CI run 32142849410, in a wasm-ld warning that had been printing on every build.

Generated with `diff -u` against the real 2.22 file. (patches/occt-freetype-tags-const.patch
records what happens when one of these is written by hand instead: it silently fails to apply
and the build fails in exactly the same place as before.)

--- a/src/temploadfem.f
+++ b/src/temploadfem.f
@@ -250,8 +250,7 @@
                enddo
 !
                call cload(xforcact(i),istep,iinc,abqtime,node,
-     &              ndirforc(i),coords,vold,mi,ntrans,trab,inotr,veold,
-     &              nmethod,idummy,ddummy,ddummy)
+     &              ndirforc(i),coords,vold,mi,ntrans,trab,inotr,veold)
                cycle
             endif
          endif
